.Dd September 22, 2026
.Dt MAIL-IMAP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mail-imap
.Nd command-line IMAP client for programmatic and AI use
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c Ar path
.Op Fl f Ar folder
.Op Fl A
.Op Fl -access-level Ar level
.Op Fl M Ar n
.Op Fl S Ar spec
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl j
.Cm command
.Op Ar args ...
.Nm
.Op Fl h | Fl V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
queries and reads email over IMAP, and can change flags, keyword tags,
and message location and the folder tree, depending on the access
level in force.
Reads use
.Li BODY.PEEK[] ,
so fetching a message never marks it
.Li \eSeen .
What
.Nm
may change is capped by the
.Ic access-level
in the config file
.Pq Sx ACCESS LEVELS ,
and every changing command names the level it needs.
.Pp
.Nm
connects to a real IMAP server over implicit TLS, STARTTLS, or plain
TCP.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Nm
takes global options anywhere on the command line, before or after
the command.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl p Ar name , Fl -profile Ar name
Which account to use, from a config that names several.
Without it the config\(aqs own
.Ic default = Qq ...
decides; a config that names none needs neither, and rejects this
option rather than ignoring it.
See
.Sx CONFIGURATION .
.It Fl c Ar path , Fl -config Ar path
Config file path.
The file must exist: a path given here is an answer, not a candidate,
so a missing one is an error rather than a reason to read another
config.
Without this option the search is
.Ev MAIL_IMAP_CONFIG ,
then
.Pa ~/.config/mail-imap.conf ,
then
.Pa /etc/mail-imap.conf ;
the first that exists is the one read, and
.Cm info
reports which.
.It Fl f Ar name , Fl -folder Ar name
Folder(s) to operate on: a literal name, or an IMAP
.Ic LIST
pattern.
A
.Ql *
in the pattern crosses the hierarchy delimiter; a
.Ql %
does not.
A mailbox whose
.Ic LIST
entry reports
.Ql NIL
for its delimiter has no hierarchy, so
.Ql %
matches there whatever
.Ql *
would.
Repeatable and comma-separated.
Default: the config's
.Ic folder
.Pq Ic INBOX ,
except for
.Cm count ,
which defaults to every mailbox.
An empty name
.Pq Fl f Ar \(aq\(aq
is refused.
.It Fl A , Fl -all-folders
Every selectable mailbox of the account; shorthand for
.Fl f Ar \(aq*\(aq .
.It Fl -access-level Ar level
Narrow what this run may change:
.Ic readonly , organize , restructure ,
or
.Ic full .
Can only lower what the config allows, never raise it.
.It Fl d , Fl -debug
Trace the exchange on stderr: the backend and access level, and which
sort and threading path were taken.
The capability list is not repeated here;
.Ic info
reports it.
.It Fl j , Fl -json
Output results as one compact JSON object on stdout.
Errors go to stderr as a JSON object with one key,
.Ic error .
.It Fl M Ar n , Fl -max Ar n
Cap search results for this run, overriding
.Ic max
from the config.
.Ar 0
is no cap, which is the default.
.It Fl S Ar spec , Fl -sort Ar spec
Sort
.Cm search Ns / Ns Cm unread
results: comma-separated criteria
.Po
.Ic uid , date , arrival , size , subject , from , to , cc
.Pc ,
first is primary, a
.Ql -
prefix reverses it
.Po e.g. Ar -date , Ar subject,-size Pc .
Overrides
.Ic sort
from the config.
Default: most recent first.
.Ic date
and
.Ic arrival
are different orderings:
.Ic date
is the message's own
.Ic Date :
header, when it was sent, and
.Ic arrival
the mailbox's internaldate, when it got here.
A message with no readable
.Ic Date :
header sorts first under
.Ic date
rather than borrowing its arrival date.
Each result line shows one of the two dates and labels it
.Po
.Ic sent
when the first criterion is
.Ic date ,
.Ic arrived
otherwise
.Pc ,
so the column the order can be read against is the one it was made
from;
.Fl j
always carries both.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print help and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
Every command that works on messages
.Po
.Cm read , thread , part , flag , tag
.Pc
takes one or more message
.Ar selection Ns s ;
see
.Sx MESSAGE SELECTIONS .
Every command also takes the folder options
.Fl f Ns / Ns Fl A .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm completion Ar shell
Print a shell completion script for
.Ar shell
.Pq Ic bash , zsh No or Ic fish
on standard output, generated from the command surface itself so it
cannot drift from the flags the binary takes.
Needs neither a configuration file nor a server.
.It Cm info
What this run can do and what the server is: the access level in
force, the hierarchy delimiter to build folder paths with, the
special-use mailboxes, and which wire path filing, sorting, and
threading take here.
Connects, sends
.Ic CAPABILITY
and
.Ic LIST ,
and changes nothing.
.It Cm folder
List mailboxes, or change the folder tree.
Every use takes a subcommand; the tree-changing ones need access level
.Ic restructure .
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Cm folder list Op Fl l Op Fl -subscribed
List mailboxes, one name per line.
.Fl -subscribed
lists only the mailboxes this account is subscribed to
.Pq an Ic LSUB ,
rather than every mailbox that exists.
.Fl l ,
.Fl -long
adds each mailbox\(aqs hierarchy delimiter and its LIST attributes
.Pq Ic \eSent , \eJunk , \eNoinferiors , ...
in parentheses after the name.
JSON output carries every field either way.
.It Cm folder create Ar folder Oo Fl -use Ar attr Oc Oo Fl -wire Oc
Create a mailbox, optionally declaring an RFC 6154 special use at
creation:
.Ic archive , drafts , junk , sent , trash , all ,
or
.Ic flagged .
.Fl -wire
takes
.Ar attr
in the form a listing prints it
.Pq Ic \eArchive
rather than as a bare word.
.It Cm folder rename Ar from Ar to
Rename a mailbox.
.Ic INBOX
is refused at every access level.
.It Cm folder subscribe Ar folder
Subscribe to a mailbox.
.It Cm folder unsubscribe Ar folder
Unsubscribe from a mailbox.
.It Cm folder delete Ar folder Op Fl -force
Delete a mailbox, permanently, with everything it holds.
Needs
.Ar access-level
.Cm full \(em not
.Cm restructure ,
which creates and renames but loses nothing.
.Ic INBOX
is refused at every level.
A mailbox that still holds messages is refused unless
.Fl -force
is given, and the refusal names how many would be destroyed.
.El
.It Cm search Ar query
Search email in the selected folder(s) with any IMAP
.Ic SEARCH
query
.Po
.Ar ALL , UNSEEN , Qq FROM bob ,
or any combination
.Pc .
Most recent first unless
.Fl S
is given.
Results are capped by
.Fl M Ns / Ns Ic max .
.It Cm read Oo Fl -raw Oc Ar selection No ...
Read the selected email(s): a short header summary, then the message\(aqs
readable text \(em its
.Ic text/plain
part, or its
.Ic text/html
part with the tags stripped \(em followed by a list of its attachments.
A message with neither says what it holds and points at
.Cm part list
rather than printing base64.
.Fl -raw
prints the MIME body verbatim instead, and no attachment list.
The header summary is read from the message\(aqs own headers, not from
the server\(aqs
.Ic ENVELOPE .
.It Cm count
Message counts and status
.Pq Ic STATUS
of the selected folder(s), or every mailbox if none is given.
Alias:
.Cm status .
.It Cm uid
List the message UIDs of the selected folder(s), one block per
folder.
.It Cm thread Ar selection No ...
List the UIDs of every message in the thread(s) containing the
selected message(s).
Uses server-side RFC 5256
.Ic THREAD
when the server advertises
.Ic THREAD=REFERENCES ,
otherwise client-side reconstruction from
.Li Message-ID Ns / Ns Li References
headers.
.It Cm unread
List unread email of the selected folder(s)
.Pq Cm search Ar UNSEEN .
.It Cm move Ar selection No ... Ar folder
File the selected email(s) into
.Ar folder ,
named last, as
.Xr mv 1
does.
.Ar folder
must already exist.
Needs access level
.Ic organize .
Uses
.Ic UID MOVE
.Pq RFC 6851
where the server has it,
.Ic UID COPY
plus
.Ic UID EXPUNGE
.Pq RFC 4315
where it does not, and refuses where the server has neither.
.It Cm copy Ar selection No ... Ar folder
Copy the selected email(s) into
.Ar folder ,
named last as
.Cm move
has it, leaving the originals where they are
.Pq Ic UID COPY , No RFC 3501 \(sc6.4.7 .
.Ar folder
must already exist.
Needs access level
.Ic organize .
Unlike
.Cm move ,
a target equal to the source folder is not refused: moving mail into
the folder it is already in would do nothing, while copying it there
makes a second copy.
.It Cm expunge Ar selection No ...
Permanently remove the selected email(s)
.Pq Ic UID EXPUNGE , No RFC 4315 .
Needs access level
.Ic full .
Only messages already marked
.Ic \eDeleted
are removed, and this command never marks any itself: if none of the
selected messages carries the flag it refuses and names
.Cm flag add Ar selection Cm deleted
as what marks them.
Needs the server to advertise
.Ic UIDPLUS ,
and refuses without it \(em a plain
.Ic EXPUNGE
would remove every
.Ic \eDeleted
message in the mailbox rather than the named ones.
There is deliberately no form that expunges a whole mailbox's
.Ic \eDeleted
set; compose it with
.Cm search Ic DELETED .
.It Cm append Ar folder Ar file Oo Fl -flag Ar names Oc Oo Fl -date Ar iso8601 Oc
Put an RFC 5322 message into
.Ar folder
.Pq an Ic APPEND ,
reading it from
.Ar file ,
or from standard input when
.Ar file
is
.Ql - .
Needs access level
.Ic full .
.Ar folder
must already exist; a server\(aqs
.Ic NO [TRYCREATE]
is reported as an error naming
.Cm folder create .
A lone LF in the message is normalised to CRLF, since an IMAP literal
is exact bytes and a message file written on this host commonly has
bare LF endings.
.Fl -flag
sets the initial flags, in the same spellings
.Cm flag add
takes.
.Fl -date
sets the INTERNALDATE, as ISO 8601
.Pq Ql 2026-09-22T18:40:11+02:00 ;
without it the INTERNALDATE is the moment of the append, and it is
never taken from the message\(aqs own
.Ic Date
header, which is the sender\(aqs clock and means something else.
The new UID is reported where the server advertises
.Ic UIDPLUS ,
and its absence is stated where it does not.
.It Cm part
List or save the MIME parts of an email.
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Cm part list Ar selection No ...
List the MIME parts of the selected email(s).
.It Cm part save Ar selection Ar part Op Fl o | Fl -out Ar file | -
Save one MIME part of one message to a file, or to standard output when
.Ar file
is
.Ql - ,
raw and undecorated, for piping.
Writing to standard output is refused under
.Fl j ,
where the JSON object and the part body would share the stream.
.It Cm part strip Ar selection Ar part No ...
Replace the named MIME part(s) of one message with a stub recording
what was there \(em filename, content type, decoded size and SHA-256 \(em
and add one
.Ic X-Mail-Imap-Stripped
header per part removed.
Needs access level
.Ic full ,
and the server must advertise
.Ic UIDPLUS .
.Pp
IMAP cannot edit a message in place, so the rebuilt message is appended
and the original removed:
.Sy the UID changes ,
and both the old and the new one are reported.
The copy keeps the original\(aqs flags and INTERNALDATE.
The part is replaced rather than removed, so part numbers do not shift
and a
.Cm part list
taken beforehand still describes the message.
The digest is of the decoded bytes \(em what
.Cm part save
would have written \(em so an attachment kept elsewhere can be matched
back to the message it came from.
.It Cm part save Ar selection Fl -all Op Fl o | Fl -out Ar dir
Save every MIME part of one message.
The files are named for the parts: each part's own filename where it has
one, otherwise
.Ar part Ns - Ns Ar N Ns .bin \(em
no extension is guessed from the content type.
.Fl o
names a directory, which must already exist; it is not created, and
.Ql -
is refused here.
An existing file is never overwritten: the run stops, naming the file
and how many parts were written before it.
.Ar selection
must name exactly one message.
Default destination: the part's filename, else
.Ar uid Ns Ar N Ns _part Ns Ar M ,
in the current directory.
The filename is taken from the message, so it is chosen by the sender:
it is used only when it is a bare file name, and one carrying a path is
refused in favour of
.Ar uid Ns Ar N Ns _part Ns Ar M .
.Fl o
is unfiltered.
.El
.It Cm flag
Enable, disable, or list the IMAP-defined message flags
.Po
.Ic \eSeen , \eAnswered , \eFlagged , \eDeleted , \eDraft
.Pc
via
.Ic UID STORE .
.Ic \eRecent
is server-managed and cannot be set.
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Cm flag list Oo Fl -wire Oc Ar selection No ...
List the flags (system flags and keyword tags) of the selected
email(s).
.It Cm flag add Oo Fl -wire Oc Ar selection No ... Ar flag No ...
Enable the given flags
.Po
.Ic seen , answered , flagged , deleted , draft
.Pc
on the selected email(s).
Needs access level
.Ic organize
to set any flag, and
.Ic full
to set
.Ic deleted .
.It Cm flag remove Oo Fl -wire Oc Ar selection No ... Ar flag No ...
Disable the given flags on the selected email(s).
Needs access level
.Ic organize .
.El
.It Cm tag
Add, remove, or list user-defined keyword tags (plain keywords, no
system flags) via
.Ic UID STORE .
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Cm tag known
List the keywords with an agreed meaning: the IANA
.Em IMAP/JMAP Keywords
registry, and the conventions no registry covers.
Needs no server.
.It Cm tag junk Ar selection No ...
Mark the selected email(s) junk: set every spelling the mailbox
keeps, clear every spelling of the opposite.
Needs access level
.Ic organize .
.It Cm tag notjunk Ar selection No ...
Mark the selected email(s) not junk, the same, the other way.
Needs access level
.Ic organize .
.It Cm tag list Oo Fl -wire Oc Ar selection No ...
List the custom keyword tags of the selected email(s); system flags
are omitted.
.It Cm tag add Oo Fl -wire Oc Ar selection No ... Ar tag No ...
Add the given keyword tags to the selected email(s).
A non-ASCII tag is NFC-composed and encoded to modified UTF-7
.Pq RFC 3501 5.1.3 .
Needs access level
.Ic organize .
.It Cm tag remove Oo Fl -wire Oc Ar selection No ... Ar tag No ...
Remove the given keyword tags from the selected email(s).
Needs access level
.Ic organize .
.El
.El
.Pp
.Fl -wire ,
on
.Cm flag
and
.Cm tag ,
means the atom exactly as the server has it.
On
.Cm add Ns / Ns Cm remove
it is what the names are taken as; on
.Cm list
it is what they are printed as.
Without it, a listing prints what
.Cm add
takes back, and
.Cm add
takes what a listing printed.
.Sh MESSAGE SELECTIONS
A message selection names messages, and optionally the folder to find
them in:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[folder::]uid-spec
.Ed
.Pp
.Ar uid-spec
is one or more comma-separated items:
.Bl -tag -width "first:N, last:Nxx" -compact
.It Ar N
one UID
.Pq Ar N No is 1 or more; 0 is refused
.It Ar N Ns - Ns Ar M
an inclusive UID range (an interval of the UID space, not a count:
it may match fewer messages than its span, or none)
.It Ar N Ns -
from
.Ar N
to the highest UID in the folder
.It Ic \&*
every message of the folder;
.Ic \&*
is only ever this, never a range endpoint
.Po
.Ar N Ns -* No and Ic \&* Ns - Ns Ar N
are both refused
.Pc
.It Ic last:N
the
.Ar N
newest messages, by UID
.Pq a count, not an interval
.It Ic first:N
the
.Ar N
oldest messages, by UID
.El
.Pp
.Ql ::
binds the folder to the spec and nothing else does; the split is at
the last
.Ql :: .
Without a folder, a selection means the folder(s) selected by
.Fl f Ns / Ns Fl A
(or the config); if several folders are selected, a bare UID is
ambiguous and refused.
A single
.Ql \&:
introduces
.Ic last:N
or
.Ic first:N
and nothing else — IMAP's own
.Ar N Ns \&: Ns Ar M
range is not accepted;
write
.Ar N Ns - Ns Ar M
instead.
.Ic INBOX
matches case-insensitively, as IMAP requires; other folder names do
not.
Folder wildcards
.Pq Ic * , %
are refused inside a selection.
Ranges and
.Ic \&*
are resolved against the folder's real UID list; every item must
match at least one message.
.Sh ACCESS LEVELS
.Ic access-level
in the config says how much of the account
.Nm
may change.
The levels are a ladder; each permits everything below it.
.Bl -tag -width "restructure"
.It Ic readonly
Nothing changes.
.It Ic organize
The default.
Set and clear flags and tags, except setting
.Ic \eDeleted ;
move mail to a folder that already exists.
.It Ic restructure
That, plus the folder tree:
.Cm folder create , rename , subscribe , unsubscribe .
.It Ic full
Everything, including setting
.Ic \eDeleted .
.El
.Pp
.Fl -access-level
on the command line can only lower what the config allows, never
raise it.
One older spelling is also accepted wherever a level is named:
.Ic read-only
for
.Ic readonly .
This is not a security boundary: the same account can be reached by
any other client with the same password.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The config file is UCL, the Universal Configuration Language.
UCL is a superset of JSON, so a config written as a JSON object
is read unchanged.
All fields except
.Ic server , username ,
and
.Ic password
have defaults.
.Bl -tag -width "access-level"
.It Ic server
IMAP host.
No default.
.It Ic port
IMAP port.
Default
.Ar 993 .
.It Ic username
Login user.
No default.
.It Ic password
Login password.
No default.
Exactly one of
.Ic password
and
.Ic password-command
must be set.
.It Ic auth
How to authenticate:
.Ql login
.Pq the default
or
.Ql xoauth2 ,
where the secret is an OAuth 2 access token rather than a password.
Under
.Ql xoauth2
the token is read from
.Ic password
or
.Ic password-command
like any other secret; acquiring it is not this program\(aqs job.
The server must advertise
.Ic AUTH=XOAUTH2 ,
and the connection is refused before anything is sent if it does not.
.It Ic timeout
Seconds to wait on the server: the dial, the TLS or STARTTLS
handshake, the greeting, a response that stops half way, and a write
the server has stopped reading.
.Ql 0
disables it.
Default:
.Ql 30 .
Resolving the name is not bounded \(em
.Xr getaddrinfo 3
takes no timeout of its own \(em so a hung resolver hangs the run.
.It Ic password-command
A command whose standard output is the password, run through
.Xr sh 1
so a pipeline works \(em
.Ql pass show mail/example ,
.Ql gpg -qd ~/.mail.gpg .
Exactly one trailing newline is stripped, so a password ending in a
space keeps it.
A non-zero exit or empty output is an error naming the exit status and
the command\(aqs standard error; there is no fall back to an empty
password.
There is no timeout: the command may be waiting at a passphrase prompt.
No default.
.It Ic ssl
Implicit TLS.
Default
.Ic true .
.It Ic starttls
Upgrade a plain connection with STARTTLS, used when
.Ic ssl
is
.Ic false .
Default
.Ic false .
.It Ic insecure
Accept invalid TLS certificates.
Default
.Ic false .
.It Ic folder
Default folder for commands that need one.
Default
.Ic INBOX .
.It Ic max
Cap on the number of search results fetched;
.Ar 0
is no cap.
Overridden by
.Fl M Ns / Ns Fl -max .
Default
.Ar 0 .
.It Ic sort
Default sort spec for
.Cm search Ns / Ns Cm unread ,
same format as
.Fl S Ns / Ns Fl -sort ;
overridden by
.Fl S .
Default
.Ic null .
.It Ic delimiter
The hierarchy delimiter
.Cm info
reports, overriding the server's own answer.
Advisory only; nothing rewrites a folder name.
Default
.Ic null .
.It Ic access-level
How much this tool may change; see
.Sx ACCESS LEVELS .
The key is also read as
.Ic access_level .
Default
.Ic organize .
.It Ic mock
Answer from the in-memory mock backend instead of connecting.
Development builds only: a released binary carries no mock and refuses
the command rather than reaching for the configured account.
Default
.Ic false .
.El
.Ss Several accounts in one file
A top-level block is a
.Em profile .
Any setting written beside the blocks is shared by every profile, and
a profile overrides what it names.
A shared setting is a default and not a ceiling: a profile may raise
.Ic access-level
above the shared one as well as lower it, the config file being where
the account holder states what the tool may do.
.Fl -access-level
on the command line may only narrow, and is not the same thing.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
max          = 25
access-level = readonly
default      = "work"

work {
    server   = "imap.work.example"
    username = "me@work.example"
    password = "..."
    access-level = organize
}

personal {
    server   = "imap.home.example"
    username = "me"
    password = "..."
}
.Ed
.Pp
.Cm info
names the profile in force.
A config with no blocks has no profiles, which is every config for a
single account.
A config with profiles and none selected is an error listing the names
it has, rather than a guess: choosing one means choosing which mailbox
to connect to.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev MAIL_IMAP_CONFIG
Config file path, used when
.Fl c Ns / Ns Fl -config
is not given.
Like that option it names the file outright: it is not a candidate,
and a missing one is an error.
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Replaces
.Pa ~/.config
in the search below when it is set and not empty.
.It Ev HOME
Gives
.Pa ~/.config
its meaning.
With neither this nor
.Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
set, only the machine-wide file is searched.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/mail-imap.conf
The user\(aqs config, searched first when neither
.Fl c Ns / Ns Fl -config
nor
.Ev MAIL_IMAP_CONFIG
is given.
.It Pa /etc/mail-imap.conf
The machine\(aqs config, searched when the user\(aqs is not there.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
On failure, an error is printed to stderr: plain text by default, or
under
.Fl j
a JSON object with one key,
.Ic error .
.Sh EXAMPLES
What this run can do, and what the server is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mail-imap -c incal.conf info
.Ed
.Pp
List folders, then read one message:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mail-imap -c incal.conf folder list
$ mail-imap -c incal.conf -f INBOX read 12345
.Ed
.Pp
Search, capped at 200 results, most recent first:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mail-imap -c incal.conf -f INBOX -M 200 search ALL
.Ed
.Pp
File the 20 newest messages of INBOX into Archive/2026 (needs access
level
.Ic organize ) :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mail-imap -c incal.conf -f INBOX move last:20 Archive/2026
.Ed
.Pp
Set and list flags:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mail-imap -c incal.conf -f INBOX flag add 12345 67890 flagged
$ mail-imap -c incal.conf -f INBOX flag list --wire 12345
.Ed
.Pp
The keywords with an agreed meaning are a table in the binary, so this
needs neither a server nor a config:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ mail-imap tag known
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Pa README.md ,
.Pa DESIGN.md
in the
.Nm
source distribution.
.Sh STANDARDS
.Bl -tag -width "RFC 6154" -compact
.It RFC 2047
MIME
.Pq Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions
Part Three: Message Header Extensions for Non-ASCII Text; subject
decoding.
.It RFC 3501
Internet Message Access Protocol - Version 4rev1; the base protocol,
modified UTF-7
.Pq Sx 5.1.3 ,
and the message-selection deviations noted above.
.It RFC 4315
Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP) - UIDPLUS extension; the
.Cm move
